```

Same three lines in sendVisaPickerCard (:398-400, check at :405) and sendVisaCheckoutCard (:467-469, check folded into :472). `getVisaShopSeller()` itself resolves the desk BY OWNER EMAIL, not by a hardcoded id (src/lib/visa-shop.ts:245-255): `db.seller.findFirst({ where: { owner: { email: { in: [...VISA_SHOP_OWNER_EMAILS] } } }, orderBy: { memberSince: 'asc' }, select: { id: true, name: true, ownerId: true, ... } })`, wrapped in React `cache()`, returning null (fail-closed) on any DB error. `ownerId` is typed `string | null` (visa-shop.ts:232-233) — hence the `if (!shop?.ownerId) return null` on every path.

Why it must never be an argument: the *first* of the four spoofing gates in the single write path is authorship, and it compares the passed senderId against the thread's seller — src/lib/messages.ts:251-252: `// (1) shop-side authorship` / `if (!convo.sellerProfileId || convo.sellerProfileId !== senderId) throw new Error('visa_card_author_forbidden')`. A sender *parameter* would move that decision to the caller: since these functions take NO ACTOR at all (:42-49), a caller-supplied sender is unvalidatable, and an applicant (or any other seller) could mint a real, desk-authored step/pay card. Resolving server-side means the worst a mis-authorized call can do is put a shop-authored card in a thread the shop already owns.

