So: the Supabase-side `visa_applications.user_id` is proven equal to `input.buyerProfileId` (the Prisma-side Profile id, which is the same uuid as auth.users.id) BEFORE `Conversation.visaApplicationId` is ever written. The check is bindVisaThread's "OWNERSHIP FIRST" block, dm-thread.ts:173-186:

```ts
  // ── OWNERSHIP FIRST ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  // Nothing above this point wrote, and nothing below writes until it passes. The check
  // FAILS CLOSED: a Supabase error is 'not_owner', never "probably fine".
  let owns = false
  try {
    const { data, error } = await getVisaDb()
      .from('visa_applications').select('user_id').eq('id', applicationId).maybeSingle()
    if (error) throw error
    owns = !!buyerProfileId && (data as { user_id?: string } | null)?.user_id === buyerProfileId
  } catch (e) {
    console.error('[visa-dm] ownership check failed', e)
    return { ok: false, error: 'not_owner' }
  }
  if (!owns) return { ok: false, error: 'not_owner' }
```

Three properties to preserve: (a) it is positioned so nothing has written yet and nothing writes until it passes; (b) it fails CLOSED — a Supabase error becomes `not_owner`, never a pass; (c) it is a strict `===` on the raw column, plus `!!buyerProfileId` so an empty id can't match an empty/undefined `user_id`.

This is why bindVisaThread is the ONE self-gating function in the file (:55-57): "a binding is only written after visa_applications.user_id == the buyer (rule 2), so bindVisaThread is the one function that IS self-gating and may be called with a buyerProfileId straight off the session". Everything else takes no actor.

A second ownership-shaped check follows, for already-bound cases (:188-200): the case belongs to this buyer, but if the thread that already names it belongs to someone else, that is corrupt state and is refused rather than "repaired":

```ts
  if (bound) {
